1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,844
Imagine your boss says,

2
00:00:01,974 --> 00:00:03,745
build me a fast online shop.

3
00:00:04,295 --> 00:00:05,233
Sounds simple.

4
00:00:05,783 --> 00:00:07,170
But what does fast mean?

5
00:00:07,720 --> 00:00:10,429
One developer hears: pages load in a blink.

6
00:00:10,979 --> 00:00:13,411
Another hears: orders ship the same day.

7
00:00:13,961 --> 00:00:15,199
Both build something.

8
00:00:15,749 --> 00:00:17,775
And neither is what the owner wanted.

9
00:00:18,825 --> 00:00:20,745
This is why requirements exist.

10
00:00:21,295 --> 00:00:25,541
In this lesson, you will write requirements that everyone reads the same way.

11
00:00:27,091 --> 00:00:29,011
First, ask who needs the system.

12
00:00:29,561 --> 00:00:31,524
These people are called stakeholders.

13
00:00:32,074 --> 00:00:33,332
The owner wants sales.

14
00:00:33,882 --> 00:00:35,696
Shoppers want it quick, and easy.

15
00:00:36,246 --> 00:00:38,507
Support staff want clear order records.

16
00:00:39,057 --> 00:00:42,428
The operations team wants something they can run, and fix.

17
00:00:42,978 --> 00:00:44,834
Each group cares about different things.

18
00:00:45,384 --> 00:00:48,626
So a good requirement starts with a conversation, not a guess.

19
00:00:50,676 --> 00:00:53,428
Teams often write requirements as user stories.

20
00:00:53,978 --> 00:00:55,791
A user story has three parts:

21
00:00:56,341 --> 00:00:57,003
who,

22
00:00:57,133 --> 00:00:57,751
what,

23
00:00:57,881 --> 00:00:58,692
and why.

24
00:00:59,242 --> 00:01:00,245
As a shopper,

25
00:01:00,375 --> 00:01:02,081
I want to search for a product,

26
00:01:02,211 --> 00:01:03,790
so that I can find it fast.

27
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:05,385
The why matters.

28
00:01:05,935 --> 00:01:08,815
It tells developers what the feature is really for.

29
00:01:10,865 --> 00:01:12,572
A story says what is wanted.

30
00:01:13,122 --> 00:01:16,044
Acceptance criteria say how we know it is done.

31
00:01:16,594 --> 00:01:19,538
A popular way to write them is: given, when, then.

32
00:01:20,088 --> 00:01:21,987
Given a shopper on the product page,

33
00:01:22,117 --> 00:01:23,717
when they search for the word mug,

34
00:01:23,847 --> 00:01:25,938
then they see only products named mug.

35
00:01:26,488 --> 00:01:29,368
Now anyone can check it, and a test can automate it.

36
00:01:29,918 --> 00:01:32,734
If you cannot check it, it is not a requirement yet.

37
00:01:34,784 --> 00:01:35,915
Now, the how well.

38
00:01:36,465 --> 00:01:39,345
Words like fast, easy, and secure are traps.

39
00:01:39,895 --> 00:01:43,201
They sound clear, but they mean different things to different people.

40
00:01:43,751 --> 00:01:45,138
So make them measurable.

41
00:01:45,688 --> 00:01:49,187
Fast becomes: the product list loads in under one second.

42
00:01:49,737 --> 00:01:53,299
Secure becomes: passwords are never stored as plain text.

43
00:01:53,849 --> 00:01:56,623
Now there is a number to check, and a rule to test.

44
00:01:58,673 --> 00:02:01,553
Non-functional requirements deserve a closer look.

45
00:02:02,103 --> 00:02:04,642
Here are five kinds you will meet again and again.

46
00:02:05,192 --> 00:02:07,282
Performance: how fast it responds.

47
00:02:07,832 --> 00:02:11,160
Security: who can do what, and how data is protected.

48
00:02:11,710 --> 00:02:14,932
Reliability: how often it works, and how it recovers.

49
00:02:15,482 --> 00:02:17,786
Usability: how easy it is to use.

50
00:02:18,336 --> 00:02:21,493
Maintainability: how easy it is to change, safely.

51
00:02:22,043 --> 00:02:25,371
Each one needs a measurable target, agreed with the owner.

52
00:02:27,421 --> 00:02:29,277
You cannot build everything at once.

53
00:02:29,827 --> 00:02:31,256
So we rank requirements.

54
00:02:31,806 --> 00:02:33,854
A simple method is called MoSCoW.

55
00:02:34,404 --> 00:02:35,194
Must have,

56
00:02:35,324 --> 00:02:36,156
should have,

57
00:02:36,286 --> 00:02:37,054
could have,

58
00:02:37,184 --> 00:02:38,656
and will not have, for now.

59
00:02:39,206 --> 00:02:41,702
For CloudCart, placing an order is a must.

60
00:02:42,252 --> 00:02:43,958
A wish list is only a could.

61
00:02:44,508 --> 00:02:47,218
Ranking keeps the first release small, and shippable.

62
00:02:49,268 --> 00:02:52,084
Let us follow one requirement, from vague to ready.

63
00:02:52,634 --> 00:02:56,261
Step one. It starts vague: customers should check out quickly.

64
00:02:56,811 --> 00:02:57,383
Step two.

65
00:02:57,383 --> 00:02:59,607
Turn it into a story: as a shopper,

66
00:02:59,607 --> 00:03:01,260
I want to pay in one step,

67
00:03:01,260 --> 00:03:02,912
so that I finish my order.

68
00:03:03,462 --> 00:03:04,177
Step three.

69
00:03:04,177 --> 00:03:06,583
Add criteria: given items in my cart,

70
00:03:06,583 --> 00:03:08,469
when I pay with a valid card,

71
00:03:08,469 --> 00:03:10,225
then I get an order number.

72
00:03:10,775 --> 00:03:15,020
Step four. Add a target: the confirmation appears within two seconds.

73
00:03:15,570 --> 00:03:18,130
Step five. Rank it. This one is a must.

74
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:21,432
Now the team can design it, build it, and test it.

75
00:03:23,482 --> 00:03:26,042
Good requirements connect to the rest of the cycle.

76
00:03:26,592 --> 00:03:28,917
Remember this rule from the CloudCart code?

77
00:03:29,467 --> 00:03:31,473
Quantity must be one to a hundred.

78
00:03:32,023 --> 00:03:34,903
A rule like this should start as a written requirement.

79
00:03:35,453 --> 00:03:38,802
Design plans for it, code enforces it, and tests check it.

80
00:03:39,352 --> 00:03:42,403
When a requirement changes, you know what else must change.

81
00:03:44,453 --> 00:03:46,565
DevOps cares about requirements too.

82
00:03:47,115 --> 00:03:50,720
Some requirements are about running the system, not only using it.

83
00:03:51,270 --> 00:03:53,894
Can we deploy it without taking the shop offline?

84
00:03:54,444 --> 00:03:56,108
Can we see it when something breaks?

85
00:03:56,658 --> 00:03:58,322
Can we roll back a bad release?

86
00:03:58,872 --> 00:04:00,984
Can we recover if a server is lost?

87
00:04:01,534 --> 00:04:06,270
Ask the operations team early, and these become requirements, not surprises.

88
00:04:08,320 --> 00:04:10,176
Watch for four common mistakes.

89
00:04:10,726 --> 00:04:12,006
One: vague words.

90
00:04:12,556 --> 00:04:14,711
Two: no owner, to make the decision.

91
00:04:15,261 --> 00:04:18,909
Three: listing features, but forgetting how well they must work.

92
00:04:19,459 --> 00:04:21,635
Four: changing requirements silently.

93
00:04:22,185 --> 00:04:24,446
Write changes down, and tell everyone.

94
00:04:26,496 --> 00:04:29,120
Before you accept a requirement, run a quick check.

95
00:04:29,670 --> 00:04:32,294
Is it clear? Would two people read it the same way?

96
00:04:32,844 --> 00:04:35,127
Is it testable? Can you prove it is met?

97
00:04:35,677 --> 00:04:38,834
Is it one thing, not three requirements in one sentence?

98
00:04:39,384 --> 00:04:41,283
Does it have an owner who agreed to it?

99
00:04:41,833 --> 00:04:44,115
Is it written down where everyone can find it?

100
00:04:44,665 --> 00:04:47,183
If any answer is no, fix it before building.

101
00:04:49,233 --> 00:04:50,108
Quick check.

102
00:04:50,658 --> 00:04:52,578
What three parts make a user story?

103
00:04:53,128 --> 00:04:55,197
What do given, when, and then describe?

104
00:04:55,747 --> 00:04:58,435
How do you turn the word fast into something you can test?

105
00:04:58,985 --> 00:05:01,481
Name two kinds of non-functional requirement.

106
00:05:02,031 --> 00:05:04,100
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

107
